      "textEn": "264\n\nMay your arms, your chest, your mouth, and may God protect your head. From the front, the wielder of the discus;\nFrom behind, Hari, the mace-bearer; and from both sides, the archer Madhuha and Khadag, Lord Vishnu, protect you.\nMay they do. The conch-bearing Uraga protect you from all dangers. Upeendra from above, Garuda on earth, and\nThe supreme being, Haldhar, protect you from all sides. Meaning: Even in the homes of farmers who were not advanced in modern methods of civilization, women\nknew how to protect their children by chanting mantras with cow dung and cow urine. From the greatest of dangers,\nThis was a simple and practical method to provide great\nsecurity. How to do this, people should know.\nThey should want to know because it is a part of Vedic civilization. Indriyani hrishikeshah prananarayanauvatu.\nOh Lord, who is seated on the white island, protect me. 24). Meaning of the word\n\nIndriyani—All the senses; Hrishikeshah—God, the protector of all the senses; Pranan—All the life forces; Narayanah—\nGod Narayan; Avatu—Protect; Shwetadvipa-patih—Lord of Shwetadvipa, Vishnu; Chittam—Heart; Manah—Mind;\nYogeshwarah—God Yogeshwar; Avatu—Grant protection. Hrishikesha, protect your senses and Narayana, protect your life force. The lord of Shwetadvipa,\nMay the protection of your mind and the Yogeshwar of your heart be with you. The one who is in the womb of the earth, that intellect, the self, the Lord, the other. Protect me, Govinda, while I sleep, protect me, Madhava. 25. Vrajanathamavyadvukuntha asinam tvam shriyah pati.\n"
